---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_71",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 71,
  "text": "[[216.8]] <b>59.  Calculi universalis investigationes April 1679</b>\n [[216.9]] <tab/> April 1679 </p>\n<p> [[217.1]] April. 1679. N^#'o. 4.\n[[217.2]] <tit2><b>Calculi universalis investigationes</b> </tit2> </p>\n<p> [[217.3]] Ad calculum universalem constituendum inveniendi sunt characteres pro terminis\n[[217.4]] quibusque, ex quibus postea inter se junctis statim cognosci queat propositionum ex\n[[217.5]] terminis conflatarum veritas. </p>\n<p> [[217.6]] Commodissimos characterum hactenus invenio esse Numeros. Sunt enim facile\n[[217.7]] tractabiles omnibusque rebus accommodari possunt, et certitudinem habent. </p>\n<p> [[217.8]] Numeri characteristici cujusque dati Termini ita fient, si numeri characteristici\n[[217.9]] terminorum ex quibus dati termini notio constituitur inter se multiplicentur, productumque\n[[217.10]] sit numerus characteristicus termini dati. </p>\n<p> [[217.11]] Itaque in omni propositione universali affirmativa vera, necesse est Numerum characteristicum\n[[217.12]] subjecti dividi posse exacte per numerum characteristicum praedicati. Ita\n[[217.13]] <i>omne aurum est metallum</i>. Item <i>omne Triangulum est trilaterum</i>; hoc enim dicit tantum\n[[217.14]] hujusmodi propositio praedicatum inesse subjecto, adeoque et numerum characteristicum\n[[217.15]] praedicati inesse numero characteristico subjecti. Inerit autem modo dicto, id est ut\n[[217.16]] multiplicantes insunt producto per multiplicationem, seu, ut divisores insunt dividendo.\n[[217.17]] Nam productus per aliquam multiplicationem semper per producentem exacte dividi\n[[217.18]] potest. </p>\n<p> [[217.19]] Porro termini sunt vel positivi vel negativi. Exempli causa Terminus positivus est\n[[217.20]] <i>homo</i>; negativus <i>non homo</i>. Fieri potest, ut terminus a parte rei positivus sit negative\n[[217.21]] expressus, ut <i>infinitum</i> (quod idem est ac <i>absolute maximum</i>), item ut negativus sit\n[[217.22]] positive expressus, ut <i>peccatum</i>, quod est <i>anomia</i>. </p>\n<p> [[217.23]] <b>Termini contradictorii</b> sunt, quorum unus est positivus alter negativus hujus\n[[217.24]] positivi, ut <i>homo</i> et <i>non homo</i>. De his regula observanda est: si duae exhibeantur\n[[218.1]] propositiones ejusdem <b>praecise subjecti</b> singularis, quorum unius unus terminorum\n[[218.2]] contradictoriorum alterius alter sit praedicatum, tunc necessario unam propositionem esse\n[[218.3]] veram et alteram falsam. Dico autem: <b>ejusdem praecise subjecti</b>, exempli causa\n[[218.4]] <i>hoc aurum est metallum, hoc aurum est non-metallum</i>. </p>\n<p> [[218.5]] Haec porro unica Propositio (nempe harum duarum <i>B est A</i> et <i>B est non A</i> una est\n[[218.6]] vera, altera falsa) continet in se has quatuor propositiones:\n [[218.7]] I. Si vera propositio est <i>B est A</i>, tunc falsa propositio est <i>B est non A</i>.\n [[218.8]] II. Si vera propositio est <i>B est non A</i>, tunc falsa propositio est <i>B est A</i>.\n [[218.9]] III. Si falsa propositio est <i>B est A</i>, tunc vera propositio est <i>B est non A</i>.\n [[218.10]] IV. Si falsa propositio est <i>B est non A</i>, tunc vera propositio est <i>B est A</i>. </p>\n<p> [[218.11]] Id est generaliter si propositionis conditionalis terminus unus sit una propositio et\n[[218.12]] unum attributum propositionis, erit terminus alter altera propositio et alterum attributum.\n[[218.13]] Propositiones scilicet sunt <i>B est A</i> et <i>B est non-A</i>, earum vero attributa sunt: <i>vera\n[[218.14]] propositio</i>, <i>falsa propositio</i>. <Fn>Definiendo falsam propositionem quae vera erit si pro praedicato ejus sumatur terminus negativus. Haec orientur ex prioribus: I. Si vera est propositio haec: <i>B est A</i>, tunc vera erit haec: <i>B est non non A</i>. II.Si vera haec propositio est <i>B est non A</i>, tunc vera propositio est haec: <i>B est non A</i>, <b>quae est identica.</b> III.Si vera est propositio haec: <i>B est non A</i>, tunc vera propositio est <i>B est non A</i>, <b>rursus identica.</b> IV. Si vera propositio est <i>B est non-non A</i>, vera propositio est <i>B est A</i>. Definitiones: <b>Termini contradictorii</b> sunt, quorum si uni praefigitur <i>non</i>, inde fit alter. Hinc sunt duo tantum, et <i>non non A</i> est idem quod <i>A</i>. <b>Propositio vera</b> est cujus praedicatum continetur in subjecto seu ei inest. Hoc est si in locum quorundam terminorum substituentur aequipollentes seu ii ex quibus componuntur ostenditur terminos simul aequipollentes praedicato omnes reperiri inter terminos aequipollentes subjecto. <b>Propositio non vera</b> seu falsa est ubi id non fit.</Fn> </p>\n<p> [[219.1]] Falsa autem propositio idem est quod non-vera. Ita ut hi duo termini <i>verum</i> et <i>falsum</i>\n[[219.2]] sint contradictorii. Unde etiam ex quibusdam harum propositionum demonstrari possunt\n[[219.3]] caeterae. Possumus et altius assurgere et sumamus exempli causa tantum hanc: </p>\n<p> [[219.4]] <b>Si propositio</b> <i>B est A</i> <b>est vera</b>, <b>tunc propositio</b> <i>B est non A</i> <b>est falsa</b>,\n[[219.5]] quam in se replicabimus. </p>\n<p> [[219.6]] Et quoniam id ipsum <i>Haec</i> <i>propositio B est A</i> rursus est subjectum propositionis, et\n[[219.7]] praedicatum est <i>vera</i>. Hinc loco subjecti <i>Haec propositio: B est A</i> scribemus <greek>b</greek>, et loco\n[[219.8]] praedicati <i>vera</i> scribemus <greek>a</greek>. Et quia <i>falsum</i> est idem quod <i>non-verum</i> (ex definitione\n[[219.9]] termini) hinc fiet talis propositio: </p>\n<p> [[219.10]] <b>Si propositio</b> <greek>b</greek> <i><b>est</b></i> <greek>a</greek> <b>est vera, tunc propositio</b> <greek>b</greek> <i><b>est non-</b></i><greek>a</greek> <b>est falsa</b>\n [[219.11]] <k>si Propositio haec</k> <tab/>(<greek>b</greek>) Propositio <tab/>est <tab/>  id est </p>\n<p> [[219.12]] <tab/><picture>303</picture> <tab/><i>B</i> <tab/><picture>204</picture>\n [[219.13]] <tab/><picture>303</picture> <tab/>(<greek>b</greek>) <k>Propositio</k> <tab/><k>est</k> </p>\n<p> [[219.14]] <k>Si Propositio haec</k> <tab/><k>est</k> <tab/><i>A</i> <tab/> <k>est vera</k>\n [[219.15]] <tab/>(<greek>a</greek>) <k>vera</k>\n[[219.16]] <tab/><picture>303</picture> <tab/><i>B</i> <tab/><picture>204</picture>\n [[219.17]] <tab/><picture>303</picture> <tab/>(<greek>b</greek>) <k>Propositio</k> <tab/><k>est</k>\n [[219.18]] <k>tunc Propositio haec</k> <tab/><k>est</k> <tab/><i>A</i> <tab/> <k>est falsa</k>\n [[219.19]] <tab/>(non-<greek>a</greek>) <k>falsa</k>\n [[219.20]] sive, ut vulgarius loquar, si verum est propositionem aliquam esse veram, falsum est eam\n[[219.21]] [esse] falsam. Id est rursus contrahendo: si propositio est vera, falsum est quod sit falsa.\n[[219.22]] Si propositio est vera, tunc haec alia propositio (<i>propositio est vera</i>) est vera. </p>\n<p> [[220.1]] In omni propositione universali affirmativa continetur praedicatum in subjecto,\n[[220.2]] adeoque dividi potest numerus characteristicus subjecti per numerum characteristicum\n[[220.3]] praedicati. </p>\n<p> [[220.4]] In omni propositione particulari affirmativa dividi potest numerus characteristicus\n[[220.5]] subjecti, per alium numerum multiplicatus, per numerum characteristicum praedicati;\n[[220.6]] ideo semper procedet propositio aliqua particularis affirmativa in terminis qui sunt pure\n[[220.7]] affirmativi et componuntur ex pure affirmativis, quia tunc nulla unquam oritur incompatibilitas. </p>\n<p> [[220.9]] Negationem alicujus termini, ut <i>non-homo</i>, non possum commode exprimere per\n[[220.10]] signum <i>minus</i>, quia id afficit totum terminum, quod hic esse non debet. Nam cum dico\n[[220.11]] <i>doctus non-prudens</i>, speciatim dico <i>esse doctum sed non prudentem</i>, possem quidem\n[[220.12]] dicere <i>non,^#6+doctus-prudens^#6-</i>, sed tunc non tantundem dico. </p>\n<p> [[220.13]] Si dicam: <i>doctus non-prudens non-justus</i>, non possumus inde facere: +<i>d</i>, ─<i>p</i>, ─<i>j</i>,\n[[220.14]] fieret enim +<i>dpj</i>. </p>\n<p> [[220.15]] Posset numero vel literae signum praefigi quale radicis quadraticae est. Nam termini\n[[220.16]] incompatibiles possunt exprimi quodammodo per numeros incommensurabiles, ut <i>a</i> et\n[[220.17]] <wn><i>a</i><wne>. Estque haec similitudo quod <i>non-non</i> dat affirmationem, ita <wn><i>a</i><wne> · <wn><i>a</i><wne> dat <i>a</i>. </p>\n<p> [[220.18]] Verum in eo hic est discrimen, nam potius id significat <wh><wn><i>a</i><wne><whe> esse <i>a</i>, nam etsi hic\n[[220.19]] componas <i>injustum injustum</i> non inde facies <i>justum</i>. </p>\n<p> [[220.20]] Si unus sit integer, alter ejus fractus, erunt incompatibiles, nam in se invicem ducti\n[[220.21]] evanescent, sed quomodo inde judicabimus propositionem impossibilem, an quia quod\n[[220.22]] inde oritur non amplius dividi potest per ullum eorum? Ita certe non poterit, nisi inde\n[[220.23]] faciendo novum fractum. Porro si velimus scire an negativus insit alicui termino dividamus\n[[220.24]] terminum per ipsum negativum, prodibit contradictorius negativi, seu numerus cui\n[[220.25]] inest affirmativus. Itaque patet non procedere divisionem.\n[[220.26]] U. A. <tab/>2<i>Omn. H est A</i>, <tab/>ergo <i>H</i> aequ. <i>rA</i>.\n [[220.27]] P. A. <tab/><i>Qu. A est H</i>, <tab/>ergo <i>rA</i> aequ. <i>vH</i>.\n [[220.28]] Possumus simpliciter pro U. N. adhibere\n [[220.29]] U. N. <tab/><i>Null. H est B</i>, <tab/>ergo <i>yH</i> inaequ. <i>rB</i>.\n [[220.30]] P. N. <tab/><i>Qu. A non est H</i>, <tab/>ergo <i>H</i> inaequ. <i>rA</i>. </p>\n<p> [[221.1]] Sed ut in numeris rem exprimamus, consideremus <i>non-Homo</i> significare quidvis\n[[221.2]] praeter hominem. Videtur autem ille esse terminus unitatis qui idem quod terminus Entis\n[[221.3]] seu cujuslibet,\n [[221.4]] <i>Non homo</i> erit <i>y - H</i>\n [[221.5]] <i>Omnis homo est non Lapis</i>, id est: <zl><i>H</i> <ne><i>non-L</i> <be> aequ. <zl><i>f</i> <ne><i>non-f</i> <be>.\n [[221.6]] <i>Qu. A est non H</i>. <tab/>Ergo <zl><i>rA</i> <ne><i>non-H</i> <be> ^&.sc <Leibniz special character> <zl><i>y</i> <ne><i>non-y</i> <be>. </p>\n<p> [[221.7]] Itaque <i>f</i> dat terminum primo incompatibilem, qui est in <i>homine</i> ejusque contradictorium\n[[221.8]] in <i>lapide</i>. </p>\n<p> [[221.9]] An sic commode pro numeris: Omnem numerum negatum separabimus ab alio per\n[[221.10]] signum <i>non-</i>, ut <i>doctus non-prudens non-justus</i> scribemus <i>d non pj</i>, et si sit solum\n[[221.11]] <i>imprudens injustus</i> scribemus: <i>1 non pj</i>. Si jam rursus negetur iste terminus <i>doctus</i>\n [[221.12]] <i>non-justus non prudens</i>, patet fieri: <i>justum prudentem indoctum</i> et scribemus <i>pj non d</i>,\n[[221.13]] quod et ita non miscemus terminos negatos affirmatis, et sciemus divisores omnes numeri\n[[221.14]] de quo agitur esse negatos. Debent autem semper aequari negati negatis, affirmati\n[[221.15]] affirmatis: in aequatione. Duo [<i>bricht ab</i>] </p>\n",
  "number": "59.",
  "title": "Calculi universalis investigationes",
  "date": "April 1679"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:59. Calculi universalis investigationes April 1679 April 1679 April. 1679. N^#'o. 4. Calculi universalis investigationes Ad calculum universalem constituendum inveniendi sunt characteres pro terminis quibusque, ex quibus postea inter se junctis statim cognosci queat propositionum ex terminis conflat...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>